      <h1 id="using-oc-compliance-plug-in" class="group scroll-mt-20">Using the oc-compliance plugin<a href="#using-oc-compliance-plug-in" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h1>
      <p class="_abstract">Although the Compliance Operator automates many of the checks and remediations for the cluster, an administrator can use the <code>oc-compliance</code> plugin to perform the full process of bringing a cluster into compliance by interacting with the Compliance Operator API and other components.</p>
      <h2 id="installing-oc-compliance_oc-compliance-plug-in-understanding" class="group scroll-mt-20">Installing the oc-compliance plugin<a href="#installing-oc-compliance_oc-compliance-plug-in-understanding" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can install the <code>oc-compliance</code> plugin to simplify compliance operations from the command line.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      Extract the <code>oc-compliance</code> image to get the <code>oc-compliance</code> binary:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ podman run --rm -v ~/.local/bin:/mnt/out:Z registry.redhat.io/compliance/oc-compliance-rhel8:stable /bin/cp /usr/bin/oc-compliance /mnt/out/"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"W0611 20:35:46.486903   11354 manifest.go:440] Chose linux/amd64 manifest from the manifest list."} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
