      </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="using-scan-setting-bindings_oc-compliance-plug-in-understanding" class="group scroll-mt-20">Using ScanSettingBinding custom resources<a href="#using-scan-setting-bindings_oc-compliance-plug-in-understanding" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>When using the <code>ScanSetting</code> and <code>ScanSettingBinding</code> custom resources (CRs) that the Compliance Operator provides, it is possible to run scans for multiple profiles while using a common set of scan options, such as <code>schedule</code>, <code>machine roles</code>, <code>tolerations</code>, and so on.</p>
      <p class="_abstract">Although that is easier than working with multiple <code>ComplianceSuite</code> or <code>ComplianceScan</code> objects, it can confuse new users.</p>
      <p>The <code>oc compliance bind</code> subcommand helps you create a <code>ScanSettingBinding</code> CR.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Run:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc compliance bind [--dry-run] -N <binding name> [-S <scansetting name>] <objtype/objname> [..<objtype/objname>]"} lang="terminal" />
      <ul>
      <li>If you omit the <code>-S</code> flag, the <code>default</code> scan setting provided by the Compliance Operator is used.</li>
      <li>The object type is the Kubernetes object type, which  can be <code>profile</code> or <code>tailoredprofile</code>. More than one object can be provided.</li>
      <li>The object name is the name of the Kubernetes resource, such as <code>.metadata.name</code>.</li>
      <li>
      Add the <code>--dry-run</code> option to display the YAML file of the objects that are created.
      <p>For example, given the following profiles and scan settings:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get profile.compliance -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                       AGE     VERSION\nocp4-cis                   3h49m   1.9.0\nocp4-cis-1-9               3h49m   1.9.0\nocp4-cis-node              3h49m   1.9.0\nocp4-cis-node-1-9          3h49m   1.9.0\nocp4-e8                    3h49m\nocp4-high                  3h49m   Revision 4\nocp4-high-node             3h49m   Revision 4\nocp4-high-node-rev-4       3h49m   Revision 4\nocp4-high-rev-4            3h49m   Revision 4\nocp4-moderate              3h49m   Revision 4\nocp4-moderate-node         3h49m   Revision 4\nocp4-moderate-node-rev-4   3h49m   Revision 4\nocp4-moderate-rev-4        3h49m   Revision 4\nocp4-nerc-cip              3h49m\nocp4-nerc-cip-node         3h49m\nocp4-pci-dss               3h49m   4.0.0\nocp4-pci-dss-3-2           3h49m   3.2.1\nocp4-pci-dss-4-0           3h49m   4.0.0\nocp4-pci-dss-node          3h49m   4.0.0\nocp4-pci-dss-node-3-2      3h49m   3.2.1\nocp4-pci-dss-node-4-0      3h49m   4.0.0\nocp4-stig                  3h49m   V2R3\nocp4-stig-node             3h49m   V2R3\nocp4-stig-node-v2r3        3h49m   V2R3\nocp4-stig-v2r3             3h49m   V2R3\nrhcos4-e8                  3h49m\nrhcos4-high                3h49m   Revision 4\nrhcos4-high-rev-4          3h49m   Revision 4\nrhcos4-moderate            3h49m   Revision 4\nrhcos4-moderate-rev-4      3h49m   Revision 4\nrhcos4-nerc-cip            3h49m\nrhcos4-stig                3h49m   V2R3\nrhcos4-stig-v2r3           3h49m   V2R3\n"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get scansettings -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                 AGE\ndefault              10m\ndefault-auto-apply   10m"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      To apply the <code>default</code> settings to the <code>ocp4-cis</code> and <code>ocp4-cis-node</code> profiles, run:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc compliance bind -N my-binding profile/ocp4-cis profile/ocp4-cis-node"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"Creating ScanSettingBinding my-binding"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <p>After the <code>ScanSettingBinding</code> CR is created, the bound profile begins scanning for both profiles with the related settings. Overall, this is the fastest way to begin scanning with the Compliance Operator.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="printing-controls_oc-compliance-plug-in-understanding" class="group scroll-mt-20">Printing controls<a href="#printing-controls_oc-compliance-plug-in-understanding" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can view a report of the compliance standards and controls that a given profile satisfies.</p>
      <p>Compliance standards are generally organized into a the following hierarchy:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>A benchmark is the top-level definition of a set of controls for a particular standard. For example, FedRAMP Moderate or Center for Internet Security (CIS) v.1.6.0.</li>
      <li>A control describes a family of requirements that must be met to be in compliance with the benchmark. For example, FedRAMP AC-01 (access control policy and procedures).</li>
      <li>A rule is a single check that is specific for the system being brought into compliance, and one or more of these rules map to a control.</li>
      <li>The Compliance Operator handles the grouping of rules into a profile for a single benchmark. It can be difficult to determine which controls that the set of rules in a profile satisfy.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      The <code>oc compliance</code> <code>controls</code> subcommand provides a report of the standards and controls that a given profile satisfies:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc compliance controls profile ocp4-cis-node"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"+-----------+----------+\n| FRAMEWORK | CONTROLS |\n+-----------+----------+\n| CIS-OCP   | 1.1.1    |\n+           +----------+\n|           | 1.1.10   |\n+           +----------+\n|           | 1.1.11   |\n+           +----------+\n..."} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="fetching-compliance-remediation-details_oc-compliance-plug-in-understanding" class="group scroll-mt-20">Fetching compliance remediation details<a href="#fetching-compliance-remediation-details_oc-compliance-plug-in-understanding" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>The Compliance Operator provides remediation objects that are used to automate the changes required to make the cluster compliant. You can use the <code>fetch-fixes</code> subcommand to help you understand exactly which configuration remediations are used.</p>
      <p class="_abstract">The <code>fetch-fixes</code> extracts the remediation objects from a profile, rule, or <code>ComplianceRemediation</code> object into a directory for you to inspect.</p>
      <Alert variant="warning" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Warning</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Use caution before applying remediations directly. Some remediations might not be applicable in bulk, such as the usbguard rules in the moderate profile. In these cases, allow the Compliance Operator to apply the rules because it addresses the dependencies and ensures that the cluster remains in a good state.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      View the remediations for a profile:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc compliance fetch-fixes profile ocp4-cis -o /tmp"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"No fixes to persist for rule 'ocp4-api-server-api-priority-flowschema-catch-all'\nNo fixes to persist for rule 'ocp4-api-server-api-priority-gate-enabled'\nNo fixes to persist for rule 'ocp4-api-server-audit-log-maxbackup'\nPersisted rule fix to /tmp/ocp4-api-server-audit-log-maxsize.yaml\nNo fixes to persist for rule 'ocp4-api-server-audit-log-path'\nNo fixes to persist for rule 'ocp4-api-server-auth-mode-no-aa'\nNo fixes to persist for rule 'ocp4-api-server-auth-mode-node'\nNo fixes to persist for rule 'ocp4-api-server-auth-mode-rbac'\nNo fixes to persist for rule 'ocp4-api-server-basic-auth'\nNo fixes to persist for rule 'ocp4-api-server-bind-address'\nNo fixes to persist for rule 'ocp4-api-server-client-ca'\nPersisted rule fix to /tmp/ocp4-api-server-encryption-provider-cipher.yaml\nPersisted rule fix to /tmp/ocp4-api-server-encryption-provider-config.yaml"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The <code>No fixes to persist</code> warning is expected whenever there are rules in a profile that do not have a corresponding remediation, because either the rule cannot be remediated automatically or a remediation was not provided.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      You can view a sample of the YAML file. The <code>head</code> command will show you the first 10 lines:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ head /tmp/ocp4-api-server-audit-log-maxsize.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: config.openshift.io/v1\nkind: APIServer\nmetadata:\n  name: cluster\nspec:\n  maximumFileSizeMegabytes: 100"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      View the remediation from a <code>ComplianceRemediation</code> object created after a scan:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get complianceremediations -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                                             STATE\nocp4-cis-api-server-encryption-provider-cipher   NotApplied\nocp4-cis-api-server-encryption-provider-config   NotApplied"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc compliance fetch-fixes complianceremediations ocp4-cis-api-server-encryption-provider-cipher -o /tmp"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"Persisted compliance remediation fix to /tmp/ocp4-cis-api-server-encryption-provider-cipher.yaml"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      You can view a sample of the YAML file. The <code>head</code> command will show you the first 10 lines:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ head /tmp/ocp4-cis-api-server-encryption-provider-cipher.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: config.openshift.io/v1\nkind: APIServer\nmetadata:\n  name: cluster\nspec:\n  encryption:\n    type: aescbc"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="viewing-compliance-remediation-details_oc-compliance-plug-in-understanding" class="group scroll-mt-20">Viewing ComplianceCheckResult object details<a href="#viewing-compliance-remediation-details_oc-compliance-plug-in-understanding" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">When scans are finished running, <code>ComplianceCheckResult</code> objects are created for the individual scan rules. You can use the <code>view-result</code> subcommand to provide a human-readable output of the <code>ComplianceCheckResult</code> object details.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      Run:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc compliance view-result ocp4-cis-scheduler-no-bind-address"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ul>
